.TH MASTO 1
.SH NAME
masto \- Mastodon client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B masto
.I credfile
.RB [ timeline
.RB [ -n
.IR nnn ]
.RB [ -max
.IR id ]
.RB [ -v ]]
.br
.B masto
.I credfile
.B post
.IR text\ ...
.br
.B masto
.I credfile
.B reply
.I id
.IR text\ ...
.br
.B masto
.I credfile
.B fav
.I id
.br
.B masto
.I credfile
.B boost
.I id
.br
.B masto
.I credfile
.B follow
.I acct
.br
.B masto
.I credfile
.B notifications
.RB [ -n
.IR nnn ]
.br
.B masto
.I credfile
.B profile
.RI [ acct ]
.br
.B masto
.I credfile
.B thread
.I id
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Masto
is a Mastodon client. Every invocation reads the instance hostname
and personal access token from
.IR credfile ,
then runs one command against that instance;
.B timeline
is the default.
.PP
.B Timeline
prints the home timeline, one line per status, oldest last; a boost
names both accounts:
.EX
\&@alice@mastodon.social: what a nice day
\&@dan@example.social boosted @erin@example.social: the original
.EE
.B -n
sets how many statuses (1\-40, default 30);
.B -v
follows each status with an indented line carrying its
.I id
\(em the value
.BR reply ,
.B fav
and
.B boost
take. When statuses were printed, the last line names the
.B -max
.I id
that continues below this page.
.PP
.B Post
publishes
.I text
(the remaining arguments, joined with spaces) as a new status and
prints its id and URL.
.B Reply
posts under the status named by
.IR id .
.B Fav
and
.B boost
favourite and reblog it.
.B Follow
looks
.I acct
up on the instance and follows the account.
.PP
.B Notifications
lists recent notifications, one per line: the type
.RB ( favourite ,
.BR reblog ,
.BR follow ,
.BR mention ,\ ...),
who, and the status text when one is attached.
.B Profile
prints an account's profile \(em the caller's own (via
.BR verify_credentials )
when
.I acct
is omitted: account, id, display name, note, and counts.
.B Thread
prints the conversation around the status named by
.IR id :
ancestors first, the subject marked with
.RB \(lq "> " \(rq,
descendants indented beneath.
.PP
.I Credfile
is one of three things:
.TP
a file path
holding exactly two lines: the instance hostname with no scheme
(\c
.B mastodon.social
, not
.BR https://mastodon.social ),
then a personal access token \(em from that instance's own Settings >
Development > New Application page.
.B read
scope suffices for the reading commands;
.B write
and
.B follow
cover the rest. This skips Mastodon's OAuth2 authorization-code
exchange entirely: a personal access token is a real, first-class
credential Mastodon itself issues for exactly this case, not a
workaround. Keep this file readable only by its owner; nothing here
enforces that.
.TP
.B -
ask
.IR factotum (4)
for a
.B "proto=pass service=masto"
key
.RB ( user= instance
or
.BR user= acct@instance,
.BI !password= token
\(em the instance is whatever follows the last
.BR @ ,
or the whole
.B user
value when there is none), loaded there from the user's
.IR secstore (1)
file at login rather than from a plaintext file. Add one with, e.g.,
.EX
echo 'key proto=pass service=masto user=INSTANCE !password=TOKEN' >/mnt/factotum/ctl
.EE
.TP
a keyspec fragment
(anything containing
.BR = )
appended to the factotum query to pick among several
.B masto
keys.
.PP
Either way
.B masto
never places the token on a command line (where a
.IR ps (1)
listing could see it) or in any diagnostic output.
.PP
Status text arrives from Mastodon as a small fragment of HTML
(typically wrapped in
.BR <p> ,
with
.B <br>
for line breaks and
.B <a>
/
.B <span>
around mentions and hashtags); this strips tags, treating
.B <br>
and
.B </p>
as line separators rather than dropping them outright (which would
otherwise glue adjacent words together with no space between them),
and decodes the handful of entities Mastodon's own renderer emits
.RI ( &amp; ,
.IR &lt; ,
.IR &gt; ,
.IR &quot; ,
.IR &apos; / &#39; )
rather than pull in a real HTML parser for a few paragraphs of text.
.SH TRANSPORT
Same as
.IR bsky (1):
HTTPS through
.IR https (2),
this tree's own certificate-verifying TLS 1.2 client \(em the
original host-\c
.IR curl (1)\c
-through-\c
.B #C
stopgap is gone. See
.IR bsky (1)'s
TRANSPORT section, not repeated here.
